em just wasn't designed for this  311 was a friday morning  the two top executives at the nuclear  power plants owner and operator tokyo  electric power or tepco were out of the  office  tepco chairman katsumata tsunahisa was  on a junket in beijing with other  industry executives the ceo shimizu  was with his family in the historic city  of nara sightseeing  once the disaster unfolded and he  recognized that the optics were terrible  shimizu struggled to get back to tokyo  train service was briefly suspended so  he tried to hire a private helicopter  but japanese aviation law prohibits  private helicopters from taking off  after 7 pm  so  he tried to get help from japan's  self-defense forces its military command  and he took off from nagoya at 11 pm in  a c-130 transport  but as soon as he was airborne the  defense ministry had the airplane turn  around because authorities wanted to  prioritize the evacuation of victims of  the earthquake and the tsunami  when the ceo finally got to tokyo at 10  am the next morning he began to realize  the full scope of the disaster  a day later he attended a press  conference and then  he disappeared from public view for a  month  he left mid-level officials to deal with  the disaster and handle questions from  the media there were widespread rumors  that chimizu had committed suicide or  suffered a nervous breakdown  but and here's the problem he came  through the crisis quite well  unlike hundreds of thousands of people  in fukushima prefecture people who were  forced to abandon their homes and the  entire region  shimizu did resign as the ceo of tepco  but he was promptly appointed to the  board of fuji oil whose largest  shareholder is tepco  so if you wanted to pick a poster child  for the dysfunction of the japanese old  boy network shimizu would be an ideal  choice he'd worked for a decade at tepco  joining the company right out of college  and he'd risen through its ranks despite  a questionable safety record at another  