 of public safety and  trust  now even before the disaster at  fukushima support for khan and his  democratic party of japan had cratered  khan's approval rating was a dismal 18  it was so low it even rebounded a bit  after 311  but the scope of the disaster and  government mismanagement opened the door  for a resurgence of japan's liberal  democratic party its long-time  powerhouse  the ldp decisively won the december 2012  elections  but once the ldp returned to power it  pretended everything was fine the new  prime minister abe shinzo returned to  the status quo  you see the ldp wanted cheap electricity  to promote the profitability of japan's  industries  and it was convinced that it could  overcome  public concerns about nuclear power even  after fukushima  but the ldp was wrong  its attempt to restart japan's nuclear  power plants ran into furious opposition  public opinion polls showed that upwards  of 80 percent of the population wanted  to reduce or even eliminate nuclear  power plants  public demonstrations attracted as many  as 200 000 protesters  and the opposition cut across the  political spectrum  the nobel prize-winning author oe  quenzabaro who had long criticized  nuclear weapons was among the leading  activists no surprise there  the surprising member of the  anti-nuclear movement was kobayashi  yoshinori  a manga author  kobayashi is best known for his  right-wing revisionist take on japanese  history  kobayashi's popular graphic novels  insist that world war ii in the pacific  resulted from japan's attempts to  liberate east asia from western  imperialism and so the war was not an  act of japanese aggression  but now he joined  old leftists like oe as a fervent  opponent of restarting the reactors  the breadth of the anti-nuclear movement  blunted industry plans it was so strong  that whereas before 311  japan's 54 reactors had produced roughly  25 percent of the country's electricity  after fukushima only four reactors  produced about four percent of its  electricity  but the liberal democratic party went  ahead with its plans to minimize  alternatives to nuclear power it  eliminated the previous government's  subsidies for renewable energy  especially solar power  as a result japan has become more  dependent on imported fossil fuels  especially natural gas but also coal  and japan fell behind its targets for  limiting greenhouse gas emissions  much japanese progress towards its goal  of reducing emissions has come from  reduced industrial output from a slow  economy not from increased efficiency  even a decade after fukushima japan had  no sensible plan to meet electricity  demand while reducing greenhouse gases  the government and the major utilities  insist that the answer has to be nuclear  power but the electorate wants the  reduction or elimination of nuclear  power so the ldp's plan is in essence to  hope that voters will forget about 311  so the nuclear village can restart more  power plants and that's not so much a  plan as a fantasy  now for an old japan hand like me that  situation is bizarre because it once  seemed like old japan inc business  industrial cooperation was ready for the  green revolution  the first detailed international  agreement on climate change was  concluded in 1997 in kyoto and it's  known accordingly as the kyoto protocol  that protocol built on the 1992 united  nations framework convention on climate  change  but under the kyoto protocol the world's  major industrialized countries committed  to specific reductions in greenhouse  gases  japan committed to a six percent  reduction in greenhouse gases  and it seemed like japan inc saw the  kyoto protocols as a business  opportunity  for example toyota accelerated  development of the first practical  consumer hybrid vehicle the prius so it  could debut the car when the kyoto  accords were signed  now yes the kyoto protocol was a  challenge to reduce greenhouse gases  but it was also a massive opportunity  by the early 2020s 