 single in 2035 it will be a society  largely without conventional families  in japan as elsewhere the decline in  family formation is driven largely by  economic insecurity  women who follow conventional gender  roles want men with conventional stable  jobs  and those jobs are less common in japan  after the bursting of the economic  bubble in 1990  and men who follow conventional gender  roles aren't interested in marrying and  assuming the responsibilities of a  family that they might not be able to  support  the decline of lifetime employment and  the rise of a freelance economy have  made marriage and family formation less  attractive  the prospect of japan as a single  society rather than a family society  panics social and political  conservatives  now there are limits to what government  policy can accomplish but there are some  pretty good models for how to raise  marriage and fertility rates  these include business incentives to  create steady full-time jobs labor laws  that raise the minimum wage laws that  increase job security subsidies for new  families strong family leave polities  and gender equality in the workforce so  that women can contribute more to family  income  but the liberal democratic party is too  traditional and too beholden to industry  to embrace those kinds of changes  on the contrary it blocks even symbolic  gestures  such as letting women in japan keep  their original family names after  marriage  and it doesn't help when japan's  political elite keeps sounding like  grumpy old male chauvinists  for example the head of the organizing  committee for the 2020 tokyo olympics  announced that he doesn't like to  include women in meetings because they  talk too much  that guy mori yoshido was already known  as a gaff-prone sexist so why at 83 was  he the best choice for such a visible  job  like shimizu at tepco the old boy  network helps its own  so what's the alternative  some cultural critics are attempting to  put an optimistic spin 