however japan was no  longer a clear leader in green vehicles  or green technologies  the government's ministry of  international trade and industry did  choose to promote hydrogen fuel cells  and that's potentially a remarkable  technology  but that requires a massive  infrastructure hydrogen refueling  stations are expensive whereas it's  relatively cheap to add an electric  charging station to a parking garage so  fuel cells might be a great long-term  strategy  but its short-term prospects seem  limited  and it takes energy to make and compress  the hydrogen for the fuel cells  so using fossil fuels to make the fuel  cells doesn't help much in greenhouse  gas emissions  in fact the whole hydrogen fuel cell  plan really works only if you guessed it  only if japan reopens those 50 shuttered  nuclear power plants  so japan's political establishment  stuck  before the 1990 financial crisis japan's  political establishment could ignore  much of public opinion  it was confident that voters would be  satisfied with continued economic growth  but after decades of sluggish growth  that strategy doesn't work  and the japanese political establishment  doesn't have a plan b  so in that broader sense 311 was a  beyond design basis event for the entire  political establishment  here's another area where japan seems  stuck family policy  japan has experienced population  shrinkage since 2010  its young people aren't getting married  they aren't starting families they  aren't making enough children to keep  the population from declining  so by most projections the japanese  population will shrink for the  foreseeable future of course most  developed countries would shrink without  immigration in 2018 the united states  the united kingdom sweden denmark france  australia and new zealand all had  fertility rates of about 1.7 children  per woman of childbearing age  that's not enough to replace two adults  but in japan the rate was 1.4  and by some estimates half of japan will  be single in 2035 it will be a society  largely without conventional families  in japan as elsewhere the decline in  family formation is driven largely by  economic insecurity  women who follow conventional gender  roles want men with conventional stable  jobs  and those jobs are less common in japan  after the bursting of the economic  bubble in 1990  and men who follow conventional gender  roles aren't interested in marrying and  assuming the responsibilities of a  family that they might not be able to  support  the decline of lifetime employment and  the rise of a freelance economy have  made marriage and family formation less  attractive  the prospect of japan as a single  society rather than a family society  panics social and political  conservatives  now there are limits to what government  policy can accomplish but there are some  pretty good models for how to raise  marriage and fertility rates  these include business incentives to  create steady full-time jobs labor laws  that raise the minimum wage laws that  increase job security subsidies for new  families strong family leave polities  and gender equality in the workforce so  that women can contribute more to family  income  but the liberal democratic party is too  traditional and too beholden to industry  to embrace those kinds of changes  on the contrary it blocks even symbolic  gestures  such as letting women in japan keep  their original family names after  marriage  and it doesn't help when japan's  political elite keeps sounding like  grumpy old male chauvinists  for example the head of the organizing  committee for the 2020 tokyo olympics  announced that he doesn't like to  include women in meetings because they  talk too much  that guy mori yoshido was already known  as a gaff-prone sexist so why at 83 was  he the best choice for such a visible  job  like shimizu at tepco the old boy  network helps its own  so what's the alternative  some cultural critics are attempting to  put an optimistic spin on these issues  arakawa kazihisa a researcher and  marketing consultant has looked to  japan's past to find a more promising  future  arakawa argues that what he calls super  solo societies can produce vibrant new  cultures  in the 1600s for example the small  village of edo which is now tokyo  rapidly turned into a huge city as  samurai poured in from around japan to  serve the shogun and daimyo lords  many of these samurai had left their  families at home so the result was a  huge pool of single men who needed  lodging and food and they wanted  entertainment  so that super solo society produced much  of what we now call traditional japanese  food culture including sushi tempura and  soba noodles  each of these foods acquired its modern  forms in the booming capital of edo and  then diffused to the rest of the country  the demand for entertainment also  produced kabuki theater so  traditional japanese culture have little  to do with traditional families  so maybe family values are overrated  maybe japan's solo society will produce  an exciting new future  and if you check travel magazines japan  is commonly rated as a great place for  solo travelers that's based on things  like 24 hour stores that sell everything  from salads to underwear to sim cards it  all makes japan an easy place to thrive  as a single traveler  or as a freelancing digital nomad  and as many unmarried young japanese  might attest they might say hey i might  not have enough money or a stable job to  start a family but i've got more than  enough to have fun and travel with my  friends  now in 2016  the winner of japan's most prestigious  literary prize was a short novel called  konbini  that's translated as convenience store  woman although it's actually convenience  store human  it's the tim of a disaffected woman who  finds happiness in her mundane routine  as a convenience store employee she  doesn't want to marry she doesn't even  really want a social life she just wants  her routine at the convenience store  it won the akutagawa prize and it sold  millions of copies and it was adapted as  a radio drama  the author murata sayaka clearly struck  a nerve as one reader tweeted what is  normal anyway and why should people who  aren't normal have to suffer  now to judge by literary prizes and  movie adaptations japan is full of  people finding meaning beyond  traditional families there are widows on  quests of self-discovery there are  secretive bitcoin miners  but it's hard for traditionalists to  embrace that future  it requires giving up the idea of  japanese empire 1.0 japan's military  empire in the early 20th century  and also empire 2.0  japan has an economic superpower in the  second half of the 20th century  and forsaking those visions of empire  accepting japan as a slower growth solo  society that requires a new vision of  japan  and depending on one's perspective that  new vision of japan it's either  exhilarating or terrifying  [Music]  you
 

